<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Waterloo Helmet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 150–50 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Waterloo Bridge Helmet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> Helmet is a Celtic bronze horned helmet of about 150–50 BCE, dredged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">River Thames</a> near Waterloo Bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1868 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Made of riveted bronze sheets with two conical horns, it bears repoussé decoration in the La Tène style. Too fragile for battle, it was probably made for ceremony and is thought to have been thrown into the river as a votive offering. It is the only actual horned helmet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/waterloo-helmet.jpg" alt="Waterloo Helmet" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ealdgyth · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britishmuseumwaterloohelmet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
